      "category": "engine",
      "title": "B6/B7 4.2L V8 Timing Chain Tensioner Failure",
      "description": "The 4.2L V8 in the B6 and B7 S4 has timing chain guides and tensioners at the back of the engine (against the firewall) that wear prematurely. Replacement requires engine removal, making this one of the most expensive common repairs.",
      "solution": "Diagnose by scanning for cam/crank correlation faults (commonly P0011, P0021, P0016-P0018), listening for prolonged chain rattle on cold start, and confirming camshaft adjustment values with VCDS or equivalent; severe cases may also show worn guide material in the oil pan. The proper repair is engine/transmission removal and replacement of the full rear timing service components: primary and secondary chain tensioners, chain guides/rails, timing chains as needed, cam adjuster hardware, seals/gaskets, and often related wear items such as valve cover gaskets and clutch or rear main seal while access is available. Because the timing set is mounted at the rear of the 4.2L V8, this is a labor-intensive job typically requiring precise locking tools and timing setup, with independent shop costs commonly around $4,000-$7,000 and dealer repairs often higher. Continuing to drive with startup rattle or timing faults risks chain jump and catastrophic valve-to-piston engine damage.",
      "severity": "high",
      "confidence": "medium",
      "symptoms": [
        "Rattling on cold start",
        "Check engine light",
        "Timing codes",
        "Engine stall",
        "Rough running"
      ],
      "affectedSystems": [],
      "dtcCodes": [
